{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 19, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Tripoli and the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Humberge of Toron. Raymond III was married to Eschiva of Bures and they had one daughter Hodierna of Tripoli. \n\nHe was known for his military campaigns against the Muslim forces in the region. Raymond III was a key figure in the Third Crusade and was a vassal of King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem. He was also involved in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and played a significant role in the internal conflicts of the kingdom.\n\n* **Early Life and Accession** *\nRaymond III became the Count of Tripoli in 1152 following the death of his father. He was just a child at the time and his mother Humberge of Toron served as regent until he came of age. During his early years as count Raymond III faced several challenges including the threat of Muslim forces and the internal conflicts within the kingdom.\n\n* **Military Campaigns** *\nRaymond III was a skilled military leader and played a key role in several battles against the Muslim forces. He was involved in the siege of the city of Darbaid and also participated in the Battle of Montgisard. Raymond III also led a military campaign against the Muslim forces in the region of Transjordan.\n\n* **Relationship with King Baldwin IV** *\nRaymond III had a complex relationship with King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem. The two men were vassals of each other and worked together on several occasions including during the Third Crusade. However Raymond III also had disagreements with King Baldwin IV over issues such as the treatment of the Templars and the distribution of land within the kingdom.\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 without an heir and his daughter Hodierna of Tripoli inherited his title. However she was forced to surrender the county to the M